  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 155 PM MDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nLarge areas of moderate to heavy rain with embedded storms will \ncontinue Tuesday as the H5 low shifts east across southeast AZ and \nthe remnant circulation from Polo slides northeast into southern NM. \n12Z models are aggressive with QPF as the two systems merge over the \nregion Tuesday and Tuesday night. Surface-based instability is the \ngreatest across eastern NM where storms with torrential rainfall are \npossible. The flood threat will be significant with a 55% chance of \nflash flooding within 25km of any point across eastern NM. River \nflooding may begin over the region Tuesday night with widespread \n48-hr rainfall amounts of 1 to 3\" expected by sunrise Wednesday. \nThis will come on top of already saturated soils from recent \nrecord rainfall.\n\nAn atypical weather feature for our region with tropical remnants\nis the potential for windy conditions on Tuesday. MOS guidance is\nhinting at patchy sustained winds in the 25 to 35kt range. The \n12Z NAM actually pulls the remnant circulation center from Polo \nnorthward from south-central NM across the ABQ metro area into \nnortheast NM Tuesday with 700mb winds near 50kt. The surface low \ncrosses near the ABQ metro with a pressure near 989mb. This may \nlead to a period of stronger winds in the high terrain which has \npotential to down trees in saturated soils. The 12Z RRFS indicates\nsouth and southeast wind gusts of 35-45 mph near the remnant \ncirculation center. This is a low probability event but warrants \nsome attention.\n\nBy Wednesday, there is disagreement beginning to develop with regard \nto how the entire system exits the region. The 12Z EC and Canadian \nare still consistent with a slower low progression while the NAM and \nGFS are more progressive. Either way, it appears there may be rain \nlingering over northern and eastern NM for at least part of the day \nWednesday. The ECMWF actually keeps precip lingering over northeast \nNM into Thursday. Locally greater than 5\" of rain is possible by\nWednesday night, especially over northern and northeast NM. Flash\nflooding would be most severe in areas with training storms, recent\nheavy rain from last week, and around burn scars.",
